   "oldText": "## Cross-divider anomaly-count task (observed in task-9ec6dc05)

Evidence: two demonstrations used full-width and full-height uniform divider lines to split a grid into rectangular rooms. Each room had a dominant background and scattered cells of one other color. The output had one cell per room. In both demos, an output cell was `1` exactly when that room contained exactly two scattered non-background cells; otherwise it was the input's background color (7 in demo 1, 0 in demo 2). The divider color is excluded from room counts. Demo 1's 14x7 grid became 3x2; demo 2's 9x9 grid became 2x2.

Operational procedure for similar inputs: identify full uniform divider rows/columns; treat the resulting enclosed rectangular sections as rooms; for each room count cells that differ from its dominant background while ignoring divider lines. If count is exactly 2, emit 1; otherwise emit the background color. The divider color is excluded from room counts. Validate the output dimensions equal the number of room bands by number of room columns.

This is an observed pattern, not a universal ARC rule; request an informative demo if marker/background roles or the trigger count are ambiguous.",
   "newText": "## Cross-divider exact-two marker-count task (observed in task-9ec6dc05)

Evidence: two demonstrations and an accepted test used full uniform separator rows/columns to split grids into rectangular tiles. Each tile contains a background and sparse marker cells; separators use a third color. The output has one cell per tile, keeping the tile arrangement. In every case, output `1` exactly when the tile contains exactly two marker cells; otherwise output the background color. Divider cells are excluded from counts. Demo 1: 29x29, background 5, marker 7, divider 2, 5x5 tiles -> 5x5 output. Demo 2: 11x19, background 5, marker 6, divider 7, 3x4 tiles -> 3x4 output. Accepted test: 19x19, background 5, marker 0, divider 9, 4x4 tiles -> `[[1,1,1,5],[1,1,1,1],[5,1,5,1],[1,1,1,5]]`.

Procedure for a matching grid: identify complete uniform divider rows/columns; infer the background and sparse marker value; count markers independently in each resulting tile, ignoring dividers. Emit `1` for count exactly two, otherwise the background color. Verify output dimensions equal the number of tile row-bands by tile column-bands. Counts above two were not explicitly covered, so don't generalize beyond the exact-two condition without evidence.

This rule is evidence-specific, not universal for divider grids. Prioritize correct bookkeeping: count every marker in each tile, including its boundary row/column immediately before a divider. In this instance a first attempt missed a marker in the top-left tile; the exact recount gave the accepted result."
